// Each parent judgment is made from its own delivered promise and contents, not a child-status rule.
const decisions={
'lesson:ipsec-secure-media':[true,'Navigation and teaching unit introducing VPN/IPsec choices, ESP/AH and optional media/mode context.','The effective header is narrower than the authored media title but its VPN/IPsec promise is supported by the four-term guide, conditional service comparisons, mode discussion, three cautions and gateway/NAT model response. Both sections visibly carry beyond-textbook flags. Qualifies as introductory navigation/instruction only, not an approved core 3.2 course, working VPN, measured learning time, independently graded skill or mastered lesson.'],
'keywords:ipsec-secure-media':[true,'Four-entry guide introducing VPN, IPsec, ESP and AH in authored order.','Every definition was directly inspected. VPN is a protected logical connection in the described security context; its label alone does not specify implementation guarantees. IPsec is a suite; ESP is normally chosen when encryption/integrity are required, not a guarantee every SA enables them; AH supplies no encryption. Tabs expose each definition, not learner-specific feedback. No independent family, approved objective or applied configuration evidence.'],
'traps:ipsec-secure-media':[true,'Three cautions distinguish AH secrecy, ESP services and site-to-site connectivity.','Each caution is supported in the adjacent selected unit: AH does not encrypt, ESP is not encryption-only, and endpoint scope does not guarantee permanent uptime. Optional-context disclosure is present on the trap panel. These are reading prompts, not observations of configuration, integrity, failover or recovered weakness.'],
'lesson:risk-resilience-governance':[false,'Whole-unit header promises risk decisions, RTO/RPO, audits and checking a plan with recovery targets.','HOLD: the effective summary promises using recovery targets to check a plan, but the exact contents provide only introductory RTO/RPO keyword definitions. The five sections cover matrices/transfer, resilience labels, SLE/ALE arithmetic, assurance and governance; the authored check compares vulnerabilities, and the three delivered generated targets are risk register/SLE/ALE. No applied RTO/RPO recovery decision or four-metric comparison supports that whole-unit recovery promise. The five sections and four checks can remain bounded child proposals without qualifying this parent. Owner must narrow the promise or separately authorize teaching that fulfills it; no behavior is changed here.'],
'keywords:risk-resilience-governance':[true,'Five-entry introductory guide to a risk register, SLE, ALE, RTO and RPO.','All five definitions were freshly inspected and bound, separately from the older limited reference. Register means maintained risk/owner/treatment/status information; single-event loss differs from annual expectation; RTO is a restoration target and RPO is acceptable time-based data loss. These are concise vocabulary definitions, not calculations, a tested plan, achieved recovery, MTD/MTTR coverage or a full remediation link. The old limited-reference artifact remains unchanged and contributes zero inherited credit; this new whole-guide proposal is based on direct review, not promotion of that reference.'],
'traps:risk-resilience-governance':[true,'Three cautions about transfer, redundant components and vulnerability prioritization.','All three lines were inspected: residual and non-transferable consequences persist; a duplicate component alone does not establish fault tolerance, especially when failover is manual/untested; base CVSS severity is not complete organizational risk or priority. Untested is absence of demonstrated effectiveness rather than proof a design could never tolerate failure. No deployed control, risk acceptance, legal conclusion or actual priority is approved.'],
'deck:chapter3-data-lifecycle-protection':[false,'Seven-card collection of data states, tokenization, masking and identification treatments.','HOLD: the actual privacy-protection collection states tokens have no useful value outside a vault and anonymization makes people impossible to re-identify. Those unconditional outcomes are unsupported by its own introductory contents; useful authorized tokens and auxiliary-data linkage are counterexamples. The other five state/masking/pseudonymization cards do not repair these two delivered claims. This is an independent whole-container content judgment; existing child holds remain separate and unchanged.'],
'deck:chapter3-network-design-zones':[true,'Seven-card definition collection for screened subnets, air gaps, traffic directions, jump hosts, intranets and extranets.','All seven included bodies support the broad network-zone/navigation label. Definitions give introductory isolation and traffic orientation, not enforced ACLs, absolute isolation, a safe bastion or a measured architecture. An air gap does not remove removable-media/human paths; a screened subnet and a jump host still require policy. Qualifies as a vocabulary container only; no child approval, seven new card credits, precise scope, scenario evidence or mastery follows.'],
'deck:chapter3-resilience-recovery':[false,'Ten-card collection of fault tolerance, RAID levels, recovery sites, dispersion and load balancing.','HOLD: the availability/recovery promise is supported in part, but the geographic-dispersal body claims regions prevent one local disaster taking down the whole service without independent dependencies, usable capacity, data and traffic failover. A shared identity/DNS/control dependency defeats that guarantee. RAID and hot/warm/cold labels teach vocabulary, not a tested service recovery. This whole-container gap is visible in its own delivered content, not inferred solely from a held-child flag.'],
'deck:chapter5-awareness-training':[true,'Three-card collection naming role-based training, gamification and controlled phishing simulations.','The included examples of training methods match the container subject. Improve security behavior describes their intended purpose; this definition drill supplies no measured behavior change, reporting rate or training efficacy. Role tailoring, engagement techniques and simulations are distinct introductory terms. The visible self-rated mastered count is not an outcome experiment. Qualifies as a vocabulary/navigation proposal only, without inheriting child approvals or approving objective 5.5 as canonical scope.'],
'deck:chapter5-governance-data-roles':[false,'Seven-card collection of oversight, ownership, controller/processor and custodian/steward labels.','HOLD: a leadership/data-responsibility collection needs contextual role distinctions, but its processor body uses third-party language without the required relationship precision and its custodian/steward group supplies technical implementation work only. It does not explain governance/data-quality stewardship or declare a specific combined role. The other five governance/owner/controller definitions do not supply those missing distinctions. This parent needs bounded role context; it is not a new legal applicability determination or automatic transfer of two child holds.'],
'deck:chapter5-risk-analysis-response':[false,'Nine-card collection promising scoring, appetite, tolerance and treatment choices.','HOLD: the collection includes an unspecified numbered matrix as qualitative and defines tolerance as variation from appetite without an objective/threshold basis. A numeric matrix can encode ordinal categories, semi-quantitative scores or measured quantities; these need different interpretations. Tolerance is not blanket permission to vary outside appetite. The adjacent treatment/register cards do not resolve the scoring and tolerance distinctions that the container explicitly promises.'],
'deck:chapter5-vendor-supply-chain':[true,'Three-card collection of supplier assessment, supply-chain risk and audit-clause vocabulary.','All three bodies support the third-party review/contracts/suppliers label. Assessment can occur before and during a relationship; external components create possible exposure, not proof every supplier is compromised. A contractual audit right has agreed scope and conditions, not unrestricted access or verified supplier compliance. Qualifies only as an introductory collection; no contract interpretation, executed audit, child approval, remediation effectiveness or independent assessment.'],
'deck:cloud-mobile-hardware-attacks':[false,'Six-card attack collection with definition and authored-example study modes.','HOLD: the mobile-installation body makes untrusted sourcing intrinsic to off-store installation. A legitimate enterprise/developer installation disproves that equivalence; its adware example illustrates one malicious case without narrowing the definition. The collection otherwise gives VM escape, privilege bypass, firmware, supply-chain and physical examples, but example recall and self-ratings cannot validate compromise. This actual attack-container classification gap prevents whole-container qualification independently of the carried Side-Loading hold.'],
'deck:risk-governance-business':[false,'Thirty-two-card collection spanning risk, continuity, organizations, documents, privacy and technical identifiers.','HOLD: the collection promises risk/compliance vocabulary but PHI supplies identifiability without covered-entity/business-associate context or exclusions, and MOA/MOU supplies only a common purpose without contextual distinction. CTF/ISP/OID definitions also supply no explicit bridge to their inherited governance/privacy placement. Clear SLE/ALE/RTO/RPO/MTTR and other introductory definitions do not repair those container content/relevance gaps. No universal MOA-versus-MOU legal rule is invented and no individual/contract/health record is classified.'],
'deck:social-engineering-attacks':[false,'Thirteen-card human-targeted attack collection with examples and overlapping categories.','HOLD: the tailgating body classifies following without credentials rather than unauthorized entry, so an authorized escorted visitor is a counterexample to its promised attack classification. The broad-versus-targeted phishing phrasing also remains unclear in an introductory collection containing spear phishing/whaling. A malicious example cannot repair a universal definition or establish exclusive categories. This is a parent content judgment, not automatic inheritance of the existing two child holds.'],
'deck:threat-intel-protection':[false,'Eleven-card collection of detection, CTI exchange, email controls and protective gateways.','HOLD: the collection teaches an unqualified TAXII expansion using historical Indicator wording without a version boundary, and DKIM wording does not separate signing-domain responsibility from the purported author. That weakens its promised CTI/email vocabulary even though the other nine definitions introduce relevant tools. TAXII 2.1 and DKIM primary sources support the version/signer distinctions. No feed, signed message, detection effectiveness, protocol configuration or repaired child credit is inferred.']
};
